package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part community mystery; all chapters meet the 509-word minimum. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch38 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_orchard_of_borrowed_names",
            title = "The Orchard of Borrowed Names",
            tagline = "A faded garden sign leads two students to a better way of remembering a community.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Sign with No Names", listOf(
                    "When Ayo's class received permission to help the neighbourhood garden prepare for its twentieth anniversary, he expected ordinary jobs: repainting the gate, sorting seed packets, and carrying watering cans. Instead, he found a wooden sign leaning behind the tool shed. Its paint had faded, but the message remained clear: THIS GARDEN BELONGS TO EVERYONE. Beneath the words were twenty small rectangles where names should have been. Every rectangle had been carefully sanded blank. Ayo called his friend Lami, who was helping photograph the garden for the school newsletter. She studied the sign and noticed a tiny date carved along its lower edge: 14 June, twenty years earlier. The anniversary committee had planned to display the sign, yet nobody seemed to know who had made it or why the names were missing.",
                    "Mrs Eze, the garden coordinator, listened to their questions while she checked the seedlings. She explained that the garden had begun as a narrow strip of unused land beside the community hall. Residents had cleared it, built raised beds, and planted vegetables for families who wanted to learn. The first committee had kept a record book, but the book was believed to have been lost during a renovation. Mrs Eze remembered that the original sign had arrived before the first harvest celebration. She did not remember seeing names on it. Ayo asked whether the blank spaces might have been intentional. Mrs Eze considered this. The sign had always carried the same message, she said, but its history had become mixed with guesses repeated so often that people treated them as facts.",
                    "Lami photographed the carving without touching the old paint. At school, she enlarged the image and noticed a second line of marks hidden beneath the date. They looked like the ends of letters, not scratches. Ayo suggested that someone might have painted over a list, but Lami warned that they should not assume anyone had deliberately erased it. Weather, repairs, and changes in the wood could explain some of the marks. They wrote down what they knew and separated it from what they only suspected. Their teacher, Mr Bello, praised that approach and suggested they ask the anniversary committee for permission to examine the sign with a conservator. He also reminded them that the garden belonged to the whole neighbourhood, so any investigation should include the people who cared for it now.",
                    "That afternoon, Ayo found an old photograph on the community hall noticeboard. It showed the garden gate on opening day, with a group of people standing beside the same sign. The picture was too small to read the faces clearly, but the rectangles beneath the message seemed to contain writing. On the back, someone had written, 'For the people who made room.' No names followed. Lami placed the photograph beside her new image and spotted a detail: one corner of the sign had a painted yellow leaf, partly hidden by a climbing vine. The present sign had the same leaf. They had found a way to compare the two versions without guessing at the missing names. Their first clue was not a person or a secret location. It was a detail that could be checked."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Photograph Album", listOf(
                    "The anniversary committee gave Ayo and Lami a small table in the hall and permission to review the garden's public photographs. They were asked not to remove anything from its folder, and Mrs Eze stayed with them while they worked. The pictures showed the garden changing from a patch of rough ground into neat beds with a compost corner, a shaded bench, and a path wide enough for a wheelchair. In one photograph, a woman held the sign while a child painted its yellow leaf. In another, three neighbours carried boards together. The people appeared in many pictures, but the captions rarely named them. Most simply said 'volunteers'. Ayo began to understand why the blank spaces mattered: the garden had a visible history, but the people in it had gradually become anonymous.",
                    "Lami arranged the photographs by date and wrote a short description for each one. She noticed that the same blue watering can appeared in pictures taken years apart. It had a dent near its handle and a white star painted on its side. The can became a useful reference point because it helped them identify pictures from the first season. In one image, the can stood beside a stack of seed trays and a notebook. Ayo enlarged the notebook area, but the writing was too blurred to read. Mrs Eze recognised the notebook's green cover. She thought it might have belonged to the garden's first treasurer, a person she had heard called Auntie Rose. No one knew whether Rose was a first name, a nickname, or a title used by children.",
                    "They asked the hall caretaker whether any older records remained in storage. He checked the catalogue and found a box labelled GARDEN OPENING — COPIES. Inside were duplicate event programmes, a hand-drawn plan, and a list of donated tools. The list recorded a spade from the market association, two wheelbarrows from a building cooperative, and several packets of seeds from a school club. The names beside the donations were organisations rather than individuals. At the bottom, a note said that the original volunteers had requested a shared credit line. It did not explain what that meant. Ayo copied the note carefully, including its date and box reference, so the committee could check it later. The first records suggested that the garden had been designed as a group effort from the beginning.",
                    "Before leaving, Lami noticed a programme with a folded corner. It listed a short speech titled 'A Place We Build Together', delivered by someone identified only as R. The speech itself was missing, but the programme named the printer: a small shop on Market Road that had closed several years ago. Mrs Eze remembered the shop owner, Mr Nwosu, and said his daughter still lived nearby. The students asked whether they could contact her through the community hall, and Mrs Eze agreed to pass along their request. On the walk home, Ayo wondered if they were making too much of a few blank spaces. Lami answered that the question was not whether every person needed a plaque. It was whether the garden's history told the truth about how it had been made."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Notebook in Green", listOf(
                    "Two days later, Mrs Eze introduced them to Rina Nwosu, the former printer's daughter. Rina had kept a folder of sample programmes from her father's shop, including a copy of the garden opening programme. She remembered that the speech titled 'A Place We Build Together' had been printed twice because the first version used the wrong wording. The corrected version was in her folder. It said the garden was created by residents, local groups, and young volunteers, and that no single person should be presented as its founder. Rina explained that the committee had wanted to avoid turning a shared project into a competition. Ayo and Lami copied the text with permission and asked whether she knew anything about Auntie Rose. Rina smiled and said the name belonged to a woman who organised the first planting days.",
                    "Rina could not remember Rose's surname, but she recalled that Rose kept the green notebook shown in the photograph. She also remembered that the notebook was used to record borrowed tools and returned seed trays. Ayo wondered whether the notebook might have been stored with the garden equipment rather than the hall records. Mrs Eze agreed to help them look, but only after the current volunteers finished their scheduled work. They searched the labelled shelves together and found several old items: a cracked watering can, a bundle of plant labels, and a tin of spare screws. There was no notebook. The caretaker then suggested checking the cupboard where retired equipment was kept. The cupboard had a sign asking visitors to speak to the coordinator before opening it, so Mrs Eze unlocked it herself.",
                    "The cupboard contained a stack of empty seed boxes and a wooden tray with a green cloth wrapped around it. Inside the cloth was a notebook, its cover softened by years of use. Mrs Eze recognised the handwriting on the first page as Rose's. The notebook did not contain a list of founders. Instead, it recorded practical exchanges: who had borrowed a tool, which bed needed compost, and which family had offered seedlings. Some entries used first names, others used initials, and a few included marks beside names to show that a person preferred not to be mentioned publicly. The notebook made the garden feel less like a monument and more like a living arrangement between neighbours. Ayo and Lami asked permission to photograph selected pages, and Mrs Eze agreed after checking that no private details would be included.",
                    "Near the back, a page dated the opening week contained a note: 'The sign should leave room for every hand. If names are wanted, ask each person first.' Under it were twenty small marks matching the rectangles on the wooden sign. The students realised the spaces might never have been intended for permanent names. Perhaps they were meant for temporary notes or seasonal messages. Ayo felt embarrassed that he had assumed something had been erased. Lami was relieved to have a clue that made sense, but she pointed out that the notebook still left questions. Why had the anniversary committee expected to display the sign without knowing its meaning? And why did later photographs show writing in the spaces? They decided to follow the evidence rather than force one explanation to fit everything."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Ink beneath the Paint", listOf(
                    "With Mrs Eze's permission, the students arranged for a local conservator, Mr Adeyemi, to examine the sign. He explained that old wood and paint could be damaged by careless cleaning, so nobody should scrape or wet the surface. He used a soft light and a camera to record the layers. Under magnification, the twenty rectangles showed several different marks. Some were from pencil, some from chalk, and a few appeared to be paint. There was no single list hidden beneath the top coat. The sign had been reused many times, with different messages added and later removed. Mr Adeyemi said the safest conclusion was that the sign had served as a noticeboard as well as a welcome message. The blank spaces were part of its working life, not evidence of a deliberate attempt to erase a founder.",
                    "Ayo asked why the photographs from opening day showed writing in the rectangles. Mr Adeyemi enlarged the oldest image and explained that the writing looked like short words, not signatures. One rectangle appeared to say 'beans', another 'music', and another 'Saturday'. The sign might have carried announcements for the first harvest celebration. Lami compared the shapes with the notebook entries and found a similar letter style in a note about a Saturday planting session. The comparison was interesting but not enough to prove who had written it. They recorded the observation as a possibility, not a fact. Mr Adeyemi encouraged them to include the limits of the photograph in their exhibition. He said a careful history should show both what the evidence revealed and what it could not establish.",
                    "The committee chair, Mrs Danjuma, was pleased by the discovery but worried that the exhibition would be less exciting without a list of founders. She had hoped to announce the names of the original team at the anniversary event. Ayo explained that the notebook described a shared project and included preferences about public recognition. Mrs Danjuma listened, then admitted that she had been repeating a story she heard as a child: that one generous resident had donated the land and built the garden alone. The land records showed that the hall committee had arranged the space, while the work had been completed by many volunteers. No one had lied intentionally; a simple story had replaced a more complicated one over time. The chair agreed that the exhibition should correct the record, even if the answer was less tidy.",
                    "That evening, Lami drafted a display panel titled 'What the Sign Can Tell Us'. It explained the sign's changing uses, the notebook's shared records, and the importance of asking before publishing a person's name. Ayo added a timeline of the garden's development, using only dated photographs and documents. They left a blank panel for current visitors to write what the garden meant to them, but made the activity optional and clearly separate from the historical record. Mrs Eze suggested using removable cards instead of permanent paint. That way, people could contribute for the anniversary and take their cards home if they changed their minds. The students liked the idea. The sign had been made to welcome people, and its next chapter could do the same without pretending to know more than the evidence allowed."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Story of the Blue Watering Can", listOf(
                    "The blue watering can became the next clue when Mrs Eze brought it from a shelf and placed it beside the old photographs. Its white star had faded, but the dent near the handle matched the pictures exactly. Ayo asked who had painted the star. Mrs Eze remembered that children from the nearby primary school had decorated the garden tools during a holiday workshop. The workshop list was kept in the school's archive, so the students arranged a supervised visit with their teacher. The archive assistant found a register with the names of participating classes, but the individual pupils were not listed. A note explained that the children had worked in groups and that the school had chosen not to publish their names in the community newsletter. Once again, the records showed a deliberate choice to credit people collectively.",
                    "One teacher's note mentioned a pupil named Sade who had designed the white star. The note included no surname, and the students did not try to identify her from other records. Instead, they asked the school whether a former pupil might want to share a memory voluntarily. The school posted a short invitation on its public alumni noticeboard, explaining the project and giving a contact address for anyone who recognised the workshop. A week later, a message arrived from a woman named Sade who had attended the school. She remembered painting the can with her classmates and said the star was meant to help younger children identify the tools they were allowed to use with supervision. She was happy to share the memory but did not want her old class photograph displayed. The students respected that request.",
                    "Sade visited the garden on a Saturday and recognised the blue can immediately. She told them that the workshop had been organised by her class teacher and a group of older students. Each child had chosen a simple symbol for one tool, and the symbols had been painted so the youngest visitors could return equipment to the correct place. The star had no hidden meaning; it was a practical label that became a small piece of the garden's history. Sade also remembered a girl who had helped design the sign's yellow leaf. She thought the girl might have been called Bisi, but she was not certain. Ayo wrote down the memory with Sade's approval and marked the name as unconfirmed. Lami added a note explaining that memories could guide further research without replacing documents.",
                    "Sade's visit changed the way the students viewed the anniversary. The garden was not built by a handful of celebrated adults and then handed to everyone else. Children had helped with labels, families had shared seedlings, groups had donated tools, and volunteers had kept the place working through ordinary seasons. Many contributions were small enough to disappear from official records, yet the garden would not have been the same without them. Mrs Danjuma suggested inviting former school groups to the event, but the students advised her to make the invitation open to everyone rather than targeting people whose names appeared in old notes. Sade agreed to help prepare a simple activity where children could design removable plant markers. The blue can would be displayed beside the activity, with a caption explaining its real purpose rather than inventing a grander story."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Ledger with Two Columns", listOf(
                    "While preparing the timeline, Ayo found a reference in the notebook to a 'shared account sheet'. Mrs Eze remembered that the first garden committee had kept a record of small expenses, but she did not know where the sheet had gone. The hall caretaker searched the archive catalogue and found a folder labelled GARDEN COSTS — SUMMARY. It contained receipts for soil, timber, paint, and water fittings. The final page had two columns: 'materials' and 'time given'. The materials column listed prices. The time column listed tasks such as clearing paths, repairing beds, teaching children, and watering during school holidays. No individual totals were calculated. The committee had chosen not to turn volunteer time into a ranking. Ayo recognised the same idea as the shared credit line in the opening programme.",
                    "Lami noticed that the page included a small note about accessibility. It said that the first path had been widened after a resident who used a walking frame found the original route difficult. The change had been made before the opening day, which meant accessibility was not a later addition but part of the garden's early design. The students checked the photographs and found a picture of volunteers laying a wider path. They asked Mrs Eze whether she knew who had requested it. She remembered a man named Mr Kalu, who had attended planning meetings with his sister. The archive did not include his full name, and Mrs Eze was unsure whether he wanted to be identified. The students decided to describe the design change without naming him until they could ask.",
                    "Mrs Eze contacted Mr Kalu through the community hall's regular newsletter and passed along the students' request. He replied that he was happy to talk but preferred not to have his photograph used. During their conversation, he explained that he had suggested the wider path because his sister used a mobility aid and wanted to help with planting. He had not expected the garden to become a public example of inclusive design. He simply wanted his sister to be able to reach the beds. The committee had listened, adjusted the plan, and made the path wide enough for wheelbarrows as well. Mr Kalu said he was pleased the change had lasted, but he did not want the display to make him seem like the only person who cared about access. Many neighbours had supported the idea.",
                    "The students added a section to the exhibition called 'Small Changes That Open a Place'. It included the wider path, the tool symbols, the removable message cards, and the shared account sheet. They explained that the garden's success depended on practical choices made by many people, not on a single heroic moment. Mrs Danjuma read the draft and suggested adding a sentence about how current visitors could request improvements. Ayo wrote that suggestion on a separate card and asked the present volunteers to review it. Their project was no longer just about a sign. It was about how a community remembered its work and how it could keep making room for people who had not yet arrived."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Day the Beds Went Quiet", listOf(
                    "A week before the anniversary, a dry spell left the garden's youngest plants looking tired. The volunteers had a watering rota, but two people were away and the replacement list had not been updated. Mrs Eze asked the students to help contact the regular team through the official group notice, not by sharing private phone numbers. Ayo and Lami posted the schedule at the hall and asked visitors to sign up for a time they could manage. They also checked the rain barrel and discovered that its filter needed cleaning. The caretaker showed them how to do it safely while an adult supervised. By the end of the afternoon, the rota had enough volunteers for the week. The garden's problem was ordinary, but it reminded everyone that a shared place needed ongoing care.",
                    "During the work, Ayo found a plant label with the words 'Rose's beans' written on it. Mrs Eze said Rose had grown beans in the first season and taught children how to save seeds. The label was recent enough to read clearly, unlike the faded sign. Ayo asked whether Rose had ever been thanked publicly. Mrs Eze explained that Rose had moved away many years earlier, and the committee had lost contact with her. The notebook did not give a surname or a forwarding address. They decided not to search for personal details through unrelated sources. Instead, the committee placed a public invitation in the anniversary programme asking Rose, or anyone who had worked with her, to get in touch if they wished. The wording made clear that participation was voluntary.",
                    "Two days later, an older resident named Mr Dike visited the hall. He had worked with Rose and remembered that she was part of a neighbourhood cooking group. The group had used the garden's first harvest to teach families how to prepare affordable meals. Rose had insisted that the vegetables be shared according to need rather than given only to the people who had done the most work. Mr Dike did not know where she lived now, but he brought a recipe card she had written for a public cooking day. The card had a note at the bottom: 'Food tastes better when nobody is left out.' Mrs Eze asked whether the card could be displayed. Mr Dike agreed, provided the original stayed with him and the exhibition used a copy.",
                    "The recipe card gave the anniversary a new direction. The committee had planned speeches and a ribbon-cutting, but now they added a community table where visitors could share simple recipes and stories about the garden. No one would be required to give a name or be photographed. Lami designed a sign explaining that contributions could be anonymous and would not be used in advertising. Ayo helped arrange a display of seed packets and harvest photographs. The students also made sure the event would not interrupt the regular garden rota. The plants still needed water, the paths still needed sweeping, and the compost still needed turning. The anniversary would celebrate the garden without pretending that celebration could replace the work that kept it alive."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Invitation Returned", listOf(
                    "The public invitation produced several replies, including one from a person who had known Rose through the cooking group. She said Rose had moved to another town but sometimes visited relatives nearby. She offered to pass the invitation along, but she asked the committee not to publish Rose's new address. Mrs Eze agreed immediately. A week later, a letter arrived at the hall with no return address. It was written in careful handwriting and signed only 'Rose'. The writer said she had heard about the anniversary and was glad the garden was still open. She remembered the sign, the children painting tools, and the first harvest table. She did not want to attend the event, but she was happy for the committee to read a short message if they kept her surname private.",
                    "Rose's message explained why the sign had twenty rectangles. At the first harvest celebration, the committee had invited visitors to write a word describing what they hoped the garden would become. Some wrote 'learning', others wrote 'welcome', 'food', 'shade', 'friendship', and 'patience'. There were more words than spaces, so people added new ones over time and wiped the old ones away when the sign was needed for another event. The rectangles were not a list of names. They were a record of changing hopes. Rose said the sign had been left blank for the anniversary because the committee wanted new visitors to add their own words. The students read the letter twice. It answered the central mystery without turning the sign into a roll of famous people.",
                    "Mrs Danjuma asked Rose whether she would like her message read aloud. Rose replied that the message could be shared, but only if the committee did not try to identify her or pressure her to attend. The chair accepted those conditions and thanked her for trusting them with the memory. Ayo and Lami added the letter to the evidence list, noting that it was a personal account rather than a public record. They compared its description with the old photographs and found that the words in the rectangles matched the shapes visible in the opening-day picture. The evidence now supported a clear explanation: the sign had been a changing message board for community hopes. The students still did not know every person who had written on it, and they no longer believed they needed to.",
                    "On the afternoon before the event, the committee placed the sign in a stable display frame beside the photograph album and a copy of Rose's message. The conservator checked the frame and confirmed that it would support the wood without covering the painted leaf. A separate board held removable cards for new words. Lami added a small label: 'The spaces were made for changing messages, not permanent names. Please add a word only if you wish.' Ayo read it aloud and thought it sounded right. It told visitors what the evidence showed, left room for uncertainty, and invited them to take part without making participation a duty. The sign was ready to welcome a new generation."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Anniversary Morning", listOf(
                    "The anniversary morning began with a soft breeze and a queue of neighbours at the garden gate. Children carried seedling trays, older residents brought folding chairs, and volunteers checked that the accessible path remained clear. The committee had arranged a quiet area for anyone who wanted to rest away from the music. Ayo and Lami arrived early to check the exhibition labels. The photographs were in date order, the notebook pages were protected behind clear covers, and the old sign stood at the centre. Mrs Eze placed the blue watering can beside the tool-symbol display. Nothing had been polished into a perfect legend. The display showed ordinary records, personal memories shared with permission, and unanswered details clearly marked as unknown. Ayo felt proud that the project was honest.",
                    "Mrs Danjuma welcomed the visitors and explained that the anniversary would celebrate a shared effort rather than announce a list of founders. She thanked the people who had donated materials, cared for the beds, taught children, improved the path, and kept the garden open through difficult seasons. She also thanked those who preferred to remain unnamed. Then she invited Ayo and Lami to explain the sign. Lami described how the photographs first suggested writing beneath the message, how the conservator found different layers of chalk and paint, and how Rose's letter explained the twenty rectangles. Ayo showed the notebook and the two-column account sheet. He reminded visitors that the records were incomplete and that the project had not tried to fill every gap with a guess.",
                    "After the short presentation, visitors moved through the garden. Some recognised the old tools, while others were surprised to learn that the wide path had been part of the original design. Mr Kalu visited quietly and smiled when he saw the display. He did not ask for a special mention. Sade helped children paint removable plant markers, making sure each child could choose whether to put a name or a symbol on theirs. The community table filled with recipe cards, including a copy of Rose's message. A visitor wrote 'belonging' on one of the sign cards. Another chose 'tomorrow'. A young child wrote 'worms' and laughed when the adults nodded seriously. The cards were different, and that was the point.",
                    "Near midday, a visitor asked why the exhibition did not name every person in the photographs. Ayo explained that some records did not identify people and some people had asked not to be shown. He said the committee wanted to honour contributions without guessing or publishing details without permission. The visitor nodded and suggested that the garden could keep a voluntary memory book for future anniversaries. Mrs Eze liked the idea but proposed that it remain separate from the historical archive, so personal stories would not be mistaken for official records. The committee agreed to review the idea after the event. The question had become another useful part of the garden's story: even a celebration could teach people how to remember responsibly. As the afternoon continued, the volunteers kept the paths clear and the tables tidy, making sure every visitor could enjoy the garden at their own pace."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "A Place for New Hands", listOf(
                    "After the anniversary, the committee met to decide what should happen to the sign. Some members wanted to restore the old rectangles and keep them blank. Others wanted to add a permanent list of the organisations that had helped. Ayo and Lami presented three options: preserve the sign as it was, create a separate timeline for verified contributions, and keep removable cards for new messages. The conservator advised against repainting the old wood because the different layers were part of its history. The committee accepted the recommendation. They chose to preserve the sign, display a verified timeline nearby, and use a new board for changing messages. That decision allowed the old object to remain authentic while giving visitors a place to contribute today.",
                    "The committee also agreed to create a simple record policy. Historical information would be added only when supported by a document or a clearly attributed memory. Personal details would be shared only with permission. People could contribute anonymously, and no one would be asked to explain why they preferred privacy. The policy sounded formal, but Mrs Eze said it was simply a way to keep the garden welcoming. Lami helped turn it into a one-page guide with plain language and a contact point for corrections. Ayo suggested that every anniversary team should leave notes for the next group, including what they had checked and what remained uncertain. The committee added that idea to the guide. The garden would no longer have to rebuild its history from scattered memories.",
                    "During the next month, the students helped organise the new message board. They chose weather-resistant cards and a covered box for spare markers. They also created a small map showing where visitors could find the accessible beds, water station, tool return shelf, and quiet bench. Mrs Eze asked them to test the map with people who used the garden in different ways. One visitor suggested larger lettering. Another asked for a version that could be read from a seated position. The students made the changes and invited the volunteers to review the final design. They learned that inclusion was not a single feature added at the end. It was a habit of asking, listening, and making adjustments when people described what they needed.",
                    "One afternoon, Ayo found a new card on the board. It read 'room to learn'. Beneath it, someone had drawn a small yellow leaf. The mark reminded him of the old sign, but he did not try to identify the artist. He took a photograph of the card for the current display log and left it where it was. Lami noticed the card later and smiled. The garden was collecting new hopes in the same spirit as the first committee, but without pretending that every message needed to last forever. Ayo understood why the old rectangles had been left blank: a shared place had to make room for people who were not yet part of its story. The sign's mystery had become a practical lesson in welcome. The students agreed that this was a good ending, because it left the garden ready for the next group rather than closing its doors."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
